<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jusepe de Ribera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a>–<a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as José de Ribera, Lo Spagnoletto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jusepe de Ribera was a Spanish painter and printmaker, regarded with Zurbarán, Murillo and Velázquez as one of the major artists of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> painting. Baptised in Játiva in 1591, he was a member of the Accademia di San Luca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by 1613 and moved in 1616 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, then under Spanish rule, where he spent the rest of his life and ran a workshop with many pupils. Called Lo Spagnoletto by his contemporaries, he painted biblical and mythological scenes, brutal martyrdoms and many half-length figures of workers and beggars posed as philosophers and saints, in a stark tenebrist manner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> gave him the Cross of the Order of Christ in 1626; he died in 1652.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jusepe-de-ribera.jpg" alt="Jusepe de Ribera" width="330" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jusepe de Ribera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ribera_-_Self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Ribera's first 20 years, and it was only in 1923 that the year of his birth was positively established. He was baptised on 17 February 1591 in Játiva, about 60 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, the son of Simón Ribera, a shoemaker, and Margarita Cucó. Biographers of the 17th and 18th centuries produced much that research has since shown to be false; it was long believed he was born in 1587. The claim that he was a pupil of Francesc Ribalta in Valencia is plausible but unconfirmed.</p><p>He is recorded in Parma in 1611, paid for an altarpiece of Saint Martin, a notable commission for a foreigner of 20. He was a member of the Accademia di San Luca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by October 1613 and lived on the Via Margutta, the foreigners' quarter. Giulio Mancini wrote that he was a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a> but bolder, and that he could be lazy and extravagant and left Rome to avoid his creditors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ribera moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1616, then under Spanish rule, and in November married Caterina Azzolino, daughter of the painter Giovanni Bernardino Azzolino, whose connections helped establish him. He began to sign his work &quot;Jusepe de Ribera, español&quot; and attracted the Viceroy, the Duke of Osuna, who gave him major commissions. Most of his best prints were made between 1620 and 1626. From the late 1620s he was accepted as the leading painter of Naples and ran a workshop whose pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luca-giordano">Luca Giordano</a>. He received the Order of Christ from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> in 1626 and never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>His health began to fail from 1643 or 1644, and his output declined. In 1647–1648, during the uprising against Spanish rule, he and his family took refuge in the palace of the Viceroy. He sold his home in 1651 and was in financial hardship when he died in 1652.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Early influences were Spanish and Venetian masters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a> and Correggio. His subjects included gruesome martyrdoms and mythologies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and Marsyas, and nearly half his surviving work consists of half-length figures of saints, apostles and philosophers, painted from fishermen, dockworkers and beggars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In the early 1630s his style moved from stark tenebrism to more diffused light. He was the most significant Spanish printmaker before Goya, and his influence can be seen in Velázquez and Murillo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jusepe de Ribera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
